  "content": {
    "id": "p10-q7-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "7",
    "prompt": "The point $P\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6},\\frac12\\right)$ lies on the curve $y=2\\sin x-\\cos 2x$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p10-q7-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that the tangent at $P$ has equation $2\\sqrt3x-y=\\frac{\\pi\\sqrt3}{3}-\\frac12$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$2\\sqrt3x-y=\\frac{\\pi\\sqrt3}{3}-\\frac12$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{dy}{dx}&=2\\cos x+2\\sin 2x.\\end{align*}$$\nAt $x=\\frac{\\pi}{6}$:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{dy}{dx}&=2\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)+2\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)\\\\&=2\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}\\right)+2\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}\\right)\\\\&=2\\sqrt3.\\end{align*}$$\nThe tangent is\n$$\\begin{align*}y-\\frac12&=2\\sqrt3\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\\\\y&=2\\sqrt3x-\\frac{\\pi\\sqrt3}{3}+\\frac12\\\\2\\sqrt3x-y&=\\frac{\\pi\\sqrt3}{3}-\\frac12.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 54
      },
      {
        "id": "p10-q7-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Show that the normal at $P$ has equation $x+2\\sqrt3y=\\frac{\\pi}{6}+\\sqrt3$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x+2\\sqrt3y=\\frac{\\pi}{6}+\\sqrt3$.",
          "worked": "The normal has gradient $-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt3}$.\n$$\\begin{align*}y-\\frac12&=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt3}\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\\\\2\\sqrt3y-\\sqrt3&=-x+\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\\\x+2\\sqrt3y&=\\frac{\\pi}{6}+\\sqrt3.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      }
    ]
  },
